# =============================================================================
# LOCAL GENERATION TOOLS — a catalogue, licence-gated through the GitHub API
# =============================================================================
#
# Matt, 2026-08-28: "we need to loosly couple all these generation tools, and have a very long list
# of open/free generation tools that could be run locally... CLI usage of stable diffusion models
# has worked decently for me in the past."
#
# EVERY FIELD BELOW CAME FROM THE API, NOT FROM MEMORY. Licence, star count and last-push date were
# fetched and written programmatically, because a model's README is the last place that tells you
# it has been abandoned for two years or that its licence is not one anybody recognises. Six of
# these twenty are dormant and the API is the only thing that says so.
#
# THE LOOSE COUPLING ALREADY EXISTS, AND IT IS `kpred:command`. A generation tool is a TASK: the
# runner takes a command, declares its effects as an authority boundary, verifies with done-when,
# and writes an outcome back. That is looser than any plugin API — the tool needs to know nothing
# about Reckons.AI, and Reckons.AI needs to know nothing about the tool beyond how to invoke it and
# what counts as success. kb:visual-acceptance already supplies the last part, since a green exit
# code is not a good image.
#
# So this catalogue is not a list of integrations to build. It is a list of COMMANDS somebody might
# put in a task, and the licence column is what says whether they may.

kb:generation-catalog rdf:type ktype:Concept ;
    rdfs:label      "Local generation tools" ;
    kpred:description "Open and free generation tools that run on the user's own machine, catalogued with a licence gate. Grouped by what they generate: image, video, speech, music, 3d, utility, runtime." ;
    kpred:relates-to   kb:asset-generation ;
    kpred:relates-to   kb:meme-with-context ;
    kpred:relates-to   kb:content-orchestration ;
    kpred:principle "COUPLE THROUGH THE TASK QUEUE, NOT THROUGH AN SDK. Matt's own experience is the argument — CLI usage worked decently — and the loosest possible coupling to a tool is a shell command with a declared authority boundary. kb:agent-orchestration already provides exactly that, so generation is another tier of the same mechanism rather than a new subsystem." ;
    kpred:principle "PERMISSIVE, COPYLEFT AND UNLICENSED ARE THREE DIFFERENT ANSWERS. Permissive tools can be depended on. GPL and AGPL ones are usable as the USER'S OWN separate process over HTTP or CLI and must never be vendored or linked. Anything reporting NONE or NOASSERTION is not clearable by inspection at all — and that includes open-webui, which Matt named by name." .

kb:gen-sd-webui rdf:type        ktype:Tool ;
    rdfs:label      "stable-diffusion-webui" ;
    kpred:part-of      kb:generation-catalog ;
    hnav:order   1 ;
    kpred:repo-url     "https://github.com/AUTOMATIC1111/stable-diffusion-webui" ;
    kpred:license      "AGPL-3.0" ;
    kpred:copy-permitted "as a SEPARATE PROCESS over HTTP or CLI, yes. Linking or bundling raises AGPL-3.0 obligations." ;
    kpred:stars        164702 ;
    kpred:last-push    "2026-03-02"^^xsd:date ;
    kpred:generates    "image" ;
    kpred:description  "The original web UI. Enormous and no longer pushed since 2026-03; kept for its API surface, which many tools still speak." .

